<!--
    kuna strict fix (P9, sub-stage cast insertion; no option).

    A caller that zero-extends a sub-int value into the argument register
    (movzx edi,di) has its call input trimmed to the narrow value, because the
    register's high bits are known zero.  C promotes a sub-int expression to
    int before it becomes an argument, so the printed call `sink(a0 * 3)`
    passes the untruncated product while the binary passes zext16(a0 * 3), and
    sink reads the whole register.  The argument now carries an explicit cast
    to the unsigned type of its width whenever promotion could set its high
    bits.

      f:     lea edi,[rdi+rdi*2] / movzx edi,di  / jmp sink   (product)
      k:     add edi,1           / movzx edi,dil / jmp sink   (byte sum)
      neg_:  neg edi             / movzx edi,di  / jmp sink   (negation)
      h:     movzx edi,di        / jmp sink                   (plain input)
      g:     movsx edi,di        / jmp sink                   (sign-extended)
      d:     lea edi,[rdi+rdi*2] / movzx edi,di  / jmp sinkd  (declared uint2)
      sink:  mov [acc],edi / ret
      sinkd: mov [acc],di / ret
      bl:    movzx edi,byte [rdi+3] / jmp sink            (byte load)
      band:  movzx eax,byte [rdi+1] / and al,[rdi+2] / movzx edi,al / jmp sink
      bfit:  xor eax,eax / cmp edi,2 / setne al / add eax,6 / movzx edi,al / jmp sink
      sinkf: mov esi,edi / lea rdi,[fmt] / xor eax,eax / jmp prf   (printf)
      fwd1:  mov [acc],dil / jmp sinkf                    (clang forward)
      fwd7:  xor eax,eax / cmp dil,0xb / setge al / mov [acc],eax / jmp sinkf

    One pass, defaults only.  f, k and neg_ get the cast.  So does bl: its
    byte has no committed type and prints as char, which C sign-extends.  h
    passes an input whose promotion already zero-extends, g passes a sign
    extension, d calls a prototype that declares the narrow parameter, so C
    converts the argument itself, band ANDs two bytes typed unsigned, and
    bfit's sum is 6 or 7 whatever the input; none of those five changes.

    fwd1 and fwd7 are clang -O2's code for `void r1(signed char c) { acc8 = c;
    sinki(c); }` and `r7(signed char c) { accs = c > 10; sinki(c); }`: clang
    relies on the caller having extended the narrow parameter and passes EDI on
    untouched (`unsigned char c` compiles to the same bytes as r1).  Nothing
    zero-extended the byte, so the call keeps its promoted form even though
    fwd7's compare types the byte signed.
-->
